    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d305">

                <pb facs="0615" n="577" sameAs="frus:frus1951v03p1/pg577"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.5/9–2251: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Embassy in Turkey
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d305-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-09-22T15:00:00-04:00">September 22,
                            1951—3 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>225. Re <hi rendition="#italic">NY Times</hi> art Sept 22 stating Pres Truman has
                    assigned Harriman and Bradley to prepare way for earliest inclusion Greece and
                    Turkey in NATO and referring to their departure for Europe and Near East within
                    ten days. Info along following lines being given for background and not for
                    attribution in response to press inquiries: In the course of discussions of the
                    North Atlantic Council in Ottawa it was suggested that the US, UK and France
                    send military representatives to Turkey and Greece for preliminary discussions
                    of their participation in the NATO (after ratification by the present members of
                    their inclusion). The possibility of the US sending such a representative is now
                    being considered and if one goes it will probably be General Bradley. There are
                    no present plans for Mr. Harriman to go to Turkey or Greece.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Webb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d305-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d305-f1-content">
                <p>Message drafted by Moore. A marginal note reads: “Background statement cleared
                    with: Defense—Col Newland, White House—Mr. Tubby, RA—Mr. Elbrick.” Repeated to
                    the Embassies in Egypt, Greece, the United Kingdom, and France.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
